### Contexts — source of truth

All four providers expose **miniStore handles** (`$x`), not raw values — read them with `useMiniStore($x)` (see [State management](#state-management--ministore-first-the-usestate-replacement)). Functions like `hasPermission`/`signIn`/`setToastMessage` stay plain context members. Do not duplicate or diverge from these — they are the single source of truth.

- `applicationContext` — `$config` (`IConfiguration`) and `$theme` (`IPageTheme`) handles, plus plain `basePath` / `isSSR` / `title` / `logger`. **Theme tokens come from `$theme`** — never import from `@root/theme/theme.ts` directly. Canonical: `get_file_context("src/frontend/src/providers/application_context.tsx")`.
- `userContext` — `$user`, `$accessToken`, `$isLoggedIn`, `$hasPermissions`, `$initialized` handles, plus `hasPermission(key)` and the `signIn` / `signOut` / `signInCallback` / `signInSilentCallback` / `signOutCallback` actions. The four `use()` bootstrap promises stay (Suspense data, not state); `$accessToken.set()` drives the re-suspend cascade. Permission keys live only in the `Permissions` export. Canonical: `get_file_context("src/frontend/src/providers/user_context.tsx")`.
- `notificationContext` — `$toast` handle + `setToastMessage(message, type)`.

Reading config / user state in a component or client:

```tsx
const { $config, $theme } = useApplicationContext();
const config = useMiniStore($config);
const theme  = useMiniStore($theme);

const { $accessToken, $isLoggedIn, hasPermission } = useUserContext();
const accessToken = useMiniStore($accessToken);
const isLoggedIn  = useMiniStore($isLoggedIn);
// hasPermission(Permissions.X) is a plain function — call it directly.
```

### State management — miniStore-first (the `useState` replacement)

Cross-component state goes through **`@root/components/shared/miniStore.ts`**, NOT `useState` + Context.
A `useState` value inside a Context provider broadcasts: every consumer of that context re-renders on every write, even consumers reading unrelated fields. `miniStore` is built on React 18's `useSyncExternalStore`, so only components that call `useMiniStore($x)` re-render when `$x` changes — siblings reading other stores stay quiet. Canonical: `find_symbol("useMiniStore")`; every provider already follows this — `get_file_context` on `notification_context.tsx`, `application_context.tsx` (`$config`, `$theme`), or `user_context.tsx` (`$user`, `$accessToken`, …).

> **Note — this is for shared subscribers, owned by the shared layer.** Stores are created and exposed primarily by **providers** and **domain clients** (`clients/use[Domain]Client.ts`); leaf components are subscribers (`useMiniStore`) and writers (`$x.set`), not owners. Don't `miniStore.create` inside a page or sub-component — if state needs a store, it belongs in the provider/client that owns that concept. This is already the established pattern across the codebase: every provider (`applicationContext`, `userContext`, `notificationContext`) and every domain client (`useAxiosClient`, `useWeatherClient`, `useLocationClient`) owns its state as handles. Follow it for any new provider/client — never reintroduce a `useState`-in-Context value.

**Use miniStore when** (default for any state that isn't component-local):
- Two or more components read or write the same value.
- The value crosses provider boundaries (deep child ↔ ancestor, web-component ↔ host).
- The value lives in a Context already (drawer/modal flags, toasts, selection, theme tokens).

**Keep `useState` only when** the state is genuinely local to one component and no sibling reads or triggers it — form-field drafts, hover state, accordion open/closed, the `isLoading` flag inside one component. These disappear with the component.

**Split, don't select.** `miniStore` has **no selector API** by design — subscribers always read the whole `T`. If two pieces of state change at different rates or have different readers, make them **two stores**, not one store with a selector. If you catch yourself writing `useMiniStore($big).fieldX` in several components and ignoring the rest, `fieldX` belongs in its own store. One concept per store.

**API contract**

```tsx
import { miniStore, useMiniStore, type IMiniStore } from '@root/components/shared/miniStore.ts';

// Create — once, at the owner boundary (usually a provider). Value lives in a useRef so
// re-renders don't reset it; wrap create() in useRef so the handle stays stable across renders.
const ref    = useRef<IFoo>(initial);
const $foo   = useRef(miniStore.create(ref)).current;

// Read — re-renders only when $foo changes.
const value = useMiniStore($foo);

// Write — from anywhere (render path, handler, useEffect, window listener).
// Object.is short-circuits no-op writes, so no guards needed at call sites.
$foo.set(next);
```

The handle (`$foo`) is stable — stash it in context, prop-drill it, or attach it to a window-level object for cross web-component communication. Expose the **handle** from a provider (`$toast`), not the value, so consumers subscribe with `useMiniStore`. See `notification_context.tsx` for the canonical provider wiring (handle + memoized setter).

